## Why This Is Asked

This question probes ability to design robust, scalable schema evolution in a multi-tenant streaming system, including compatibility rules, rollouts, and testing under mixed versions.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant schema registry
- Versioned event envelopes
- Backward/forward compatibility
- Rolling upgrades and dual writes
- Data migration and validation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: coerce event to current schema version per tenant
function coerce(event, currentVersion) {
  if (event.schemaVersion === currentVersion) return event;
  if (currentVersion === 2 && event.schemaVersion === 1) {
    return { ...event, region: event.region ?? "unknown", schemaVersion: 2 };
  }
  return event;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle removing a field or changing types across tenants?
- How would you instrument metrics to detect schema drift and upgrade failures?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical TTL-state reasoning and side-input-driven re-enrichment under at-least-once guarantees.

## Key Concepts
- TTL cache per merchant
- Side-input rule versioning
- Exactly-once-like semantics via idempotent emits and commitOffsets
- Re-enrichment on rule updates

## Code Example
```javascript
function enrich(txn, ctx) {
  const rule = cache.get(txn.merchantId);
  if (!rule || rule.expiry < now()) {
    emit({ ...txn, riskScore: null, ruleVersion: rule?.ruleVersion ?? 0 });
  } else {
    emit({ ...txn, riskScore: rule.riskScore, ruleVersion: rule.ruleVersion });
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test memory pressure and eviction correctness?
- How would you handle out-of-order fraud_rules updates across partitions?

